<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<metadata>
  <!--
    This package binds the IoT SDK's own API — io.agora.iotlink — and nothing else.

    io.agora:iotsdk is an 80 MB .aar that carries a whole private copy of the RTC and Signaling
    SDKs inside it: agora-rtc-sdk.jar and agora-rtm-sdk.jar in libs/, and the matching .so files
    in jni/. That copy is an *older* drop than the one Net.Agora.Video.Android binds, and
    class-parse cannot generate it cleanly — 253 errors on the first attempt, from the same
    VideoFrame/EglBase/LocalSpatialAudioImpl shapes the Video binding removes one by one, plus
    duplicate members in the io.agora.rtc2.internal message classes that the newer drop does not
    have.

    Rather than reproduce and then maintain a second, worse copy of Net.Agora.Video.Android's
    Metadata.xml, every non-iotlink package is removed from the managed surface. Nothing is lost:
    the classes stay in the .aar and the IoT SDK calls them itself at runtime, which is the only
    thing it wants them for. A consumer who needs RtcEngine directly wants Net.Agora.Video.Android
    — and cannot have both in one app anyway, since the duplicate io.agora.rtc2.* classes fail the
    dex merge. That constraint is the reason this package exists as its own thing.
  -->
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.base']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.base.internal']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.base.internal.video']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.base.internal.voiceengine']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.common']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.common.annotation']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.mediaplayer']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.mediaplayer.data']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.mediaplayer.gl']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.audio']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.gl']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.internal']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.internal.gdp']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.live']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.proxy']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtc2.video']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtm']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtm.internal']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.rtm.jni']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.spatialaudio']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.spatialaudio.internal']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.utils']" />

  <!--
    io.agora.iotlink.rtcsdk and .sdkimpl are the SDK's own implementation of the interfaces in
    io.agora.iotlink: they subclass the bundled RTC types removed above, so what survives of them
    is a half-bound shell no consumer would construct. The interfaces they implement stay, and
    AIotAppSdkFactory is how the SDK hands out instances of them.
  -->
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.iotlink.rtcsdk']" />
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.iotlink.sdkimpl']" />

  <!--
    io.agora.iotlink.aws is the SDK's AWS IoT Core transport: its classes extend
    com.amazonaws.auth.AWSAbstractCognitoDeveloperIdentityProvider and implement
    AWSIotMqttClientStatusCallback. The AWS Android SDK is not a dependency of io.agora:iotsdk —
    its .pom declares none at all — so class-parse cannot resolve those types (BG8605) and drops
    the subclasses by itself; what is removed here is the remainder that would otherwise bind
    against a base type this package does not have.

    This matters to a consumer beyond tidiness: the device-messaging and alarm features route
    through AWS IoT Core, so an app that uses them has to add the AWS Android SDK — for which
    there is no official .NET binding. See the package description.
  -->
  <remove-node path="/api/package[@name='io.agora.iotlink.aws']" />
</metadata>
